aurora_set_async_end_frame: end a frame on the GX FIFO worker instead of draining it

Aurora 3227d76 processes the GX FIFO on a worker thread (lib/gx/fifo.cpp, ProcessingMode::Thread),
but aurora::end_frame starts with gx::fifo::drain(), which blocks the calling thread until the worker
has translated every command of the frame, and then runs the frame's end (fifo::end_frame's draw
cache reset, gx::texture::end_frame, gfx::finish, gfx::end_frame's hand-off to the render worker)
and aurora::begin_frame the next frame's gfx::begin_frame on that thread. On the Switch the worker is
slower than the game's painter, so with COS_FPS60_TEST the game thread waited ~9 ms in every
aurora_end_frame after paint B and ~5-6 ms at the split after paint A.

The patch adds an asynchronous end of frame, off by default (Aurora's behaviour is unchanged):
- aurora_set_async_end_frame(true), set between frames. aurora::end_frame then freezes ImGui's draw
  data and calls fifo::end_frame_async, which publishes the frame's commands, queues a frame job at the
  stream's end (the draw cache reset, gx::texture::end_frame, gfx::finish and the present: the frame
  handed to the render worker, the code end_frame ran after the drain, now present_frame) and returns.
  aurora::begin_frame queues gfx::begin_frame as a job at the same position (retried on the worker
  while a staging buffer cannot be mapped). The worker runs a job once it has processed the stream up
  to the job's position, and processes no command past a job not yet run, so frames stay strictly
  ordered; it only ever sees a frame's commands after that frame's begin job (the job is queued before
  any of them is published, and the worker reads sPublished before the job queue).
- end_frame_async waits while more than one ended frame is not done (kMaxFramesInFlight): the game
  thread runs at most one frame ahead of the worker. The command buffer is reset when the worker has
  processed all of it (any drain, GXDrawDone, or an end of frame finding it idle) and drained at an
  end of frame past 64 MiB.
- fifo::sync / aurora_frame_sync waits until every queued frame job has run: what a synchronous
  end_frame guaranteed (copy textures made, the frame queued to the render worker). Called by
  gfx::gpu_synchronize (so release_surface, refresh_surface and resize_swapchain, which replace the
  EFB, swapchain and surface the worker records and presents with, wait for the frames first), by
  gx::update before it applies a new viewport policy (read by the worker), by fifo::shutdown and by
  set_async_end_frame. A no-op on the FIFO worker and on the render worker (release_surface runs on
  the render worker after a lost surface) and with no frame job pending.
- drain() (GXDrawDone, AuroraGXSync, the custom draw and pass API) is unchanged: it waits until every
  command written so far is processed, frame jobs before them included.

Moved to the worker in async mode, with what else touches their state:
- gx::texture::end_frame: the texture caches and the replacement streaming publish, which the worker
  already used while processing; the game-thread replacement API locks its registry and posts cache
  clears and invalidations through atomics (applied by end_frame).
- gfx::finish / gfx::end_frame / gfx::begin_frame: the recorder and frame packets (the worker's
  already), the frame and staging slots (now acquired on the worker: the GPU's back-pressure reaches
  the game thread through the frames-in-flight wait), g_frameIndex (read by the resource and pipeline
  caches on the worker), the pipeline frame hooks.
- present_frame: webgpu::present_source and the surface configuration, which only the game thread
  changes, after gpu_synchronize's sync.
On the game thread as before: imgui::new_frame/freeze, the window and surface checks of
begin_frame, fifo's sFrameActive.

